## Two real bugs the verification surfaced

**`shipwright docs check` was exiting 1 with no output** (`7d7e26e5`). It grepped the entire repo for `<!-- AUTO: -->` markers, so a placeholder marker in a generated wiki page under `.claude/pipeline-artifacts/` was treated as a doc section. `docs_generate_section` returns 1 for an unknown id, and under `set -e` the command substitution killed the run before the status line printed — the acceptance criterion "reports 0 stale" was silently unverifiable. I scoped the scan past runtime/vendored dirs and made an unknown section count as stale with a named warning instead of aborting. Now: `6 fresh, 0 stale`, exit 0. `sw-docs-test.sh` still 18/18.

**`sw-project-detect-test` was the lone aggregate failure** (`e1c9435b`) — and it passed standalone, which is the tell. `project_detect_all` parsed `cached_at` with only the BSD form `date -j -f`. On Linux that always fails, the `|| echo 0` fallback set the epoch to 0, and `cache_age` came out as the entire Unix epoch, so **the cache never hit on Linux** — every call re-ran detection and rewrote `cached_at`. The assertion "Cached detection returns same result" only passed when both calls landed in the same wall-clock second. I try GNU `date -d` first, fall back to BSD, and require a non-zero epoch before trusting the cache. Verified the cache now survives a 2-second gap; 43/43 pass, plus `prep` and `init` which share the library.

